  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Tipping on the pre-tax amount</h2>
    <p>Traditional etiquette tips on the food and drink, not the sales tax. With the calculator's default bill of $86.40 that includes $6.40 of tax, an 18% tip is $15.55 on the full bill or $14.40 on the $80 pre-tax amount. The difference is small on one meal, about $1.15, but suggested-tip lines printed on receipts and shown on payment screens are often calculated on the after-tax total, so the "20%" option there can be more than 20% of what you ordered. Check which base the screen is using.</p>

    <h2>What's customary</h2>
    <p>In U.S. sit-down restaurants, 15% to 20% of the pre-tax bill is the widely cited range, and many diners now treat 18% to 20% as the default for good service. Customs differ for counter service, takeout, delivery, and outside the U.S., where service may be included in prices. These are norms, not rules; the calculator simply does the math for whatever percentage you choose.</p>

    <h2>Check the bill before you tip</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automatic gratuity.</strong> Many restaurants add a gratuity for larger parties. If it is already on the check, adding a full tip on top pays twice.</li>
      <li><strong>Service charges.</strong> A line labeled "service charge" or "fee" is not always passed to the staff. If it matters to you, ask.</li>
      <li><strong>Discounts and comped items.</strong> Common etiquette is to tip on what the meal would have cost before a coupon or comp, since the service was the same.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Doing it in your head</h2>
    <p>Move the decimal one place for 10%: $64.50 becomes $6.45. Double that for 20% ($12.90). For 15%, add half of the 10% figure ($6.45 + $3.23 = $9.68). For 18%, take 20% and subtract 2% ($12.90 − $1.29 = $11.61).</p>

    <h2>Splitting the check</h2>
    <p>An even split is fair when everyone ordered about the same. When orders differ a lot, have each person tip on their own share instead: use the calculator once per person with 1 in the "People" field. Even splits can leave a cent or 2 unaccounted for after rounding; round each share up.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Tip questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How much is a 20% tip on $50?</summary><p>$10. Move the decimal one place for 10% ($5) and double it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Should you tip on the total with tax?</summary><p>Traditional etiquette tips on the pre-tax amount. Tipping on the total adds the tip percentage on the tax too, which is a small amount on most bills.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I tip if there is an automatic gratuity?</summary><p>An automatic gratuity is the tip. Adding another full tip on top pays twice, though you can add more for exceptional service.</p></details>
  </section>
